Exactly. The name you give an experience can change your relationship to it—and what you can extract from it.

The same experience might be understood as:

* Trauma — something that wounded me.
* Training — something that conditioned me.
* Initiation — something that changed who I am.
* Tuition — something I paid dearly to learn.
* Adaptation — something that taught me to survive.
* Tempering — something that strengthened certain capacities.
* Wisdom — something I can now use to help myself or others.
* Inheritance — something I carry forward, perhaps without realizing it.

And here’s the interesting distinction:

An experience can produce an adaptation without that adaptation being universally beneficial.

Hypervigilance can be exceptional situational awareness in combat and exhausting at home. Distrust can be discernment in one environment and isolation in another.

The underlying capability hasn’t necessarily changed. Its relationship to the environment has.

That’s where reframing becomes more than positive thinking. It’s the ability to recognize what an experience actually produced, then decide where and how that adaptation belongs.

There is one important limit: trauma can also describe real psychological and physiological effects, regardless of what someone calls them. Renaming an injury doesn’t automatically heal it.

But neither does acknowledging an injury require making it your identity.

You can honor the wound without worshipping it. You can use the lesson without continually reliving the lesson.

And perhaps the deepest layer: something that once happened to you can eventually become something you deliberately do something with.

That’s a transformation of agency, not merely vocabulary.